Visiting the Loire Valley châteaux means crossing five centuries of French history in just a few dozen kilometres. The problem is not finding a château to see, it is choosing which ones and in what order. From the Château de la Huberdière, at Nazelles-Négron near Amboise, you sit right in the middle of the map. Here is how we suggest planning your visits.

## Which Loire Valley châteaux to visit first

If you had to keep only four or five, start with the ones that tell the Renaissance story best. Chenonceau for its gallery over the Cher, Chambord for its sheer scale and its double-helix staircase, Villandry for its gardens, Amboise and Clos Lucé for Leonardo da Vinci.

The classic mistake is trying to fit in too much. Two châteaux a day is already plenty if you want to enjoy the gardens and the surrounding villages. Better to slow down and keep time for the Loire itself, its banks, its markets and its cellars.

## The must-sees around Amboise

Amboise makes an excellent base, and it is ours too. The royal château overlooks the Loire right in the town centre, and Clos Lucé, a few steps away, holds the last home of Leonardo da Vinci and the models of his inventions. A morning at the royal château and an afternoon at Clos Lucé make an ideal first day, without ever spending long in the car.

Twenty minutes away, Chenonceau spans the Cher with its arches and Diane de Poitiers' garden. It is arguably the most photogenic château in the valley, best seen early in the morning to avoid the crowds. Allow time for the gardens and the flowering kitchen garden, which are worth the visit on their own.

## Chambord, Chaumont, Villandry

Push a little further and three visits are worth the detour. Chambord, about an hour away, impresses with its size and its hunting estate: you can hire a bicycle or a boat to explore the grounds. Chaumont-sur-Loire charms visitors with its international garden festival, renewed every year from April to November. Villandry is the benchmark for French formal gardens, best seen in spring or early summer, when the ornamental kitchen garden is at its peak.

A few distance markers from Amboise:

- Chenonceau: 20 minutes
- Chaumont-sur-Loire: 25 minutes
- Villandry: 45 minutes
- Chambord: 1 hour
- Blois: 40 minutes
- Cheverny: 50 minutes

## A three-day itinerary from Amboise

For a first stay, here is a proven pace that leaves room to breathe.

- Day 1: Amboise and Clos Lucé in the morning and afternoon, then the market and the banks of the Loire
- Day 2: Chenonceau early in the morning, lunch at Montrichard, then Chaumont-sur-Loire and its gardens
- Day 3: Villandry and its gardens, or Chambord for its sheer scale, depending on the mood

This plan avoids long drives and keeps a half-day free to unwind. You can shift it up a notch and add a fourth day for Blois and Cheverny, or for a tasting at Vouvray.

## Beyond the châteaux: vineyards and the banks of the Loire

The valley is more than its stonework. The vineyards of Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire, a few minutes from the château, welcome you into cellars often carved into the tuffeau stone. The Loire à Vélo cycle route runs along the river for kilometres of gentle paths, open to everyone. The troglodyte villages, the markets and the summer riverside guinguettes round out a stay that would feel incomplete with the monuments alone.

Taking time for these extras turns a château trip into a real stay in Touraine. It is often what visitors remember most.

## Practical tips for your visits

A few habits make the stay run more smoothly. The great châteaux open early: arriving at opening time, around nine or ten, means enjoying the rooms and the gardens before the groups. Book tickets online in advance for Chenonceau and Chambord in high season, which spares you the queue. Many sites offer a combined château-and-gardens ticket, better value than two separate entries.

Keep closing times in mind too: some monuments close earlier out of season, and the gardens of Villandry are mainly a daytime visit. To lighten the days, alternate a great château in the morning with something gentler in the afternoon, a village, a cellar, a bike ride. Touraine is better savoured at this pace than by rushing from monument to monument.

## Where to stay to visit the Loire Valley châteaux

The best base is a central address, quiet, from which you can tour without losing time on the road. That is exactly where the Château de la Huberdière sits, ten minutes from Amboise and at the centre of the valley.

Staying in a château rather than an ordinary hotel extends the experience: you come back in the evening to enclosed grounds, you dine at the host's table, you set off the next day well rested. We detail the rooms and how to plan a stay on the [stay](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) page, and our guide to [bed and breakfasts in Amboise](/en/blog/bed-and-breakfast-amboise) complements this one.

## Planning your stay

A stay of three or four nights is enough to see the essentials without rushing. You combine the great châteaux, the vineyards of Vouvray, the banks of the Loire by bike and the village markets. We know the region and we love sharing our itineraries, tailored to the season and to your pace.

To prepare your visit and check availability, head to the [stay](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) page or write to us. We will help you build the route that suits you.
